/*
 * The in-game date, in English: "LP 7 Dec, early" where the game writes
 * ＬＰ７年１２月前半.
 *
 * GameYear@ToString is the only place the game builds that string --
 * s[3678] = "%s%D年%D月%s" filled from YearTitle, Year, Month and Half -- and
 * four views draw what it returns: the turn-start banner (LPYearView), the
 * date under the turn number in the corner frame (CommonFrame@UpdateYear),
 * the ADV corner box (AdvQuestInfo) and the brave-mode caption
 * (BraveModeView, which wraps it in s[3500]). One function therefore covers
 * every one of them.
 *
 * Translating the strings instead would take two of the four with it. s[3111]
 * "前半" and s[3112] "後半" are pushed by GameYear@HalfText::get, and also by
 * ResultYearView@SetParam into "シス／戦況／年月／%s" -- which is a CG name, and
 * シス／戦況／年月／前半.ajp is a real entry in Rance10CG2. An English slot there
 * asks the war-result screen for a picture that does not exist. So the
 * strings keep their Japanese and the display is patched, the way
 * patches/card_names.jaf does it for a card Id.
 *
 * What is here is only the English. The wiring is patches/lp_date.jam, which
 * re-emits GameYear@ToString with these three called by name; it has to be
 * handed to alice-tools after this file or it stops with "Unable to resolve
 * function". docs/lp-date.md has the whole of it.
 *
 * A .jaf cannot do the job on its own, which is why there is a .jam beside it.
 * GameYear stores its four fields as the properties <YearTitle>, <Year>,
 * <Month> and <Half>, and the compiler reaches none of them: `Year` is
 * "Undefined variable", `this.Year` is "Invalid struct member name". Plain
 * members resolve -- `this.m_chapter` compiles -- but there are no plain
 * members to read here. So the month number cannot be turned into a name
 * inside an override of ToString, and the .jam passes it out to this file
 * instead.
 */

/*
 * The era. Two values reach this -- GameYear@InnerInit sets ＬＰ for chapter 1
 * and ＲＡ for chapter 2 -- and the argument is the fallback, so anything else
 * renders as it did unpatched.
 *
 * These two are s[3672] and s[3673], and nothing but the getter this reads
 * from pushes either, so they could have been translated as strings. They are
 * here because the era, the month and the half are one decision about how a
 * date reads and belong in one file.
 */
string GameYearEraName(string era)
{
	if (era == "ＬＰ") {
		return "LP";
	}
	if (era == "ＲＡ") {
		return "RA";
	}
	return era;
}

/*
 * The month, as a three-letter name. No switch -- the .jaf compiler answers
 * "switch not supported" -- and no array, because a chain of twelve is the
 * thing that certainly compiles.
 *
 * The number is the fallback rather than an empty string: the game's own
 * calendar in archives/Rance10EX_v1_04/36_ＬＰ情報.x only ever holds 1 through
 * 12, but a date built from a turn the table does not have would otherwise
 * lose the month entirely.
 */
string GameYearMonthName(int month)
{
	if (month == 1)  { return "Jan"; }
	if (month == 2)  { return "Feb"; }
	if (month == 3)  { return "Mar"; }
	if (month == 4)  { return "Apr"; }
	if (month == 5)  { return "May"; }
	if (month == 6)  { return "Jun"; }
	if (month == 7)  { return "Jul"; }
	if (month == 8)  { return "Aug"; }
	if (month == 9)  { return "Sep"; }
	if (month == 10) { return "Oct"; }
	if (month == 11) { return "Nov"; }
	if (month == 12) { return "Dec"; }
	return "%d" % month;
}

/*
 * Which half of the month. The .jam hands over Half itself rather than the
 * string GameYear@HalfText::get makes of it, so nothing here depends on
 * s[3111] and s[3112] still saying 前半 and 後半 by the time this runs.
 *
 * Everything that is not 0 is the second half, which is the test the original
 * getter makes and has to be kept: chapter 2 stores -1 for all thirteen of its
 * turns, and the game has always drawn 後半 for them. That is the game's own
 * quirk rather than a hole in the translation, and reproducing it is the point.
 */
string GameYearHalfName(int half)
{
	return half == 0 ? "early" : "late";
}
